# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: relational-cross-type-arguments-should-signal — (< 1 "a") returns nil, spec says type error
#+STATUS: DONE (clautolisp 2.2.88 / alfe 2.2.83, 2026-09-25)
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: claude/dreamy-ptolemy-uj8mzd

* Symptom

Found while fixing relational-operators-ignore-strings (clautolisp 2.2.87).
=numeric-order-p= (=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=)
returns nil for any argument list that is neither all numbers nor all
strings: =(< 1 "a")=, =(< 1 'foo)=, =(< "a" '(1))=.

The spec (ch.5, Function Entries =<= =<== =>= =>==, and "⊥ propagation")
says a non-nil cross-type argument *signals a type error*; only a =nil=
argument yields nil without error.

* Before changing it

- Probe AutoCAD and BricsCAD: =(< 1 "a")=, =(< "a" 1)=, =(< 1 'foo)=,
  =(< 'a 'b)=, =(< '(1) '(2))= — error text, or nil? The fold-to-nil
  rule was introduced for SCHMS+ loop guards on *nil*; confirm no
  production code relies on the non-nil cross-type fold.
- Classify per AGENTS.md § Dialect Divergence (case 0 if both vendors
  signal), add a =*** clautolisp= note to the spec entries if a
  deviation remains, and update =numeric-order-folds-nil-to-nil= (it
  currently asserts =(< 1 'foo)= => nil) and the compiler open-coded
  corpus entry =(> "a" 1)=.

* Resolution (clautolisp 2.2.88)

pjb asked for the spec's rule to be implemented. =numeric-order-p= now:

1. returns =T= for no argument;
2. returns nil when any argument is nil (bottom propagation). This is
   checked first, so a nil anywhere in the chain wins over a type error
   elsewhere; the =(<= 48 (car chars) 57)= loop guard is unchanged;
3. compares all-number and all-string argument lists as before;
4. otherwise signals =:invalid-comparison-argument= (message: =< expects
   numbers or strings, not a mixture or other types, got 1 "a".=). This
   covers a number against a string and any argument outside the domain
   (symbol, list), including a single one: =(< 'a)=.

Classification: case 0 (common) per the spec, which states the type
error for both vendors, so no dialect warning. The vendor confirmation
is still owed: the source carries a =SPEC-UNCERTAIN= marker and the
probe questions are in =deferred-spec-research.issue= under =< <= > >==.

Tests: =relational-operators-signal-on-incompatible-types= (new);
=numeric-order-folds-nil-to-nil= (its =(< 1 'foo) => nil= assertion is
replaced by nil-wins-over-type-error cases); the compiler's open-coded
corpus (=(> "a" 1)= is now checked as an error). The autolisp-test
corpus gives identical per-test results before and after the change
(3949 tests).

Not in scope: === and =/== still accept any types (=comparison-equal-p=
compares symbols and lists by identity, which production code relies
on), although the spec also lists =(= 1 "1")= as a type error.
